{"step_index":1316,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-10-26T12:20:51Z","input_tokens":2574,"cache_read_tokens":237795,"output_tokens":347,"content":"Understood. Because identity is strictly bound to the WireGuard keypair, cryptographic signature verification is the only valid definition:\n\n### 2-of-N Cryptographic Superadmin Dual-Approval Policy\n\n1. **Identity & Authority**:\n   - Superadmin identity is derived from and authenticated by their specific **WireGuard public key** registered in the identity store (`identity_sled` / Cozo).\n   - Any root-level operation (modifying core infrastructure, system reconfiguration, releasing/activating slots, or changing security postures) generated or proposed through the root chatbot is marked `PENDING_APPROVAL`.\n\n2. **Approval Mechanism**:\n   - The operation requires distinct cryptographic signatures from **two separate registered superadmin WireGuard identities** (a 2-of-N multi-sig requirement).\n   - Once both valid cryptographic proofs are verified against the registered keys and recorded in the audit trail, the bounded execution worker is authorized to execute the delegated change.\n   - Without both valid cryptographic proofs, execution is refused by the bridge policy interceptor.\n\n---\n\n### Implementation Scope & Confirmation\n\nTo implement and enforce this dual-superadmin policy:\n1. **Policy Definition**: Update the admission policy in `crates/op-grpc-bridge` and `deploy/config/capability-grants.json` to require two distinct superadmin approvals for root-level methods.\n2. **Key Registration**: Define the allowed registered superadmin WireGuard public keys in the identity schema.\n3. **Receipt & Audit Trail**: Persist both authorization signatures in the receipt audit trail before dispatching execution to a worker.\n\nPlease confirm if you would like me to prepare this policy implementation now."}
